# Third-party checkout for the graphrag MCP server, which was dropped
# (it duplicated reasoning.search_steps -- see FIXES-2026-09-15.md). Nothing
# references it any more; safe to delete, kept only to avoid re-downloading.
harness/neo4j-mcp-experiments/
.mon*
pod-artifacts/

# make_fixture.py's scratch clones -- one shallow clone per screened
# candidate, regenerable with `scripts/make_fixture.py` and 2.4 GB on disk.
# They were untracked and unignored, so `git status` listed thirteen of them
# on every run.
fixtures/.*-build/
# Per-attempt trees kept by run_experiment.sh for after-the-fact scoring.
runs/*-artifacts/
.backup/
# enola's code-graph snapshot. `ingest_code_graph` writes it to a scratch
# directory now; ignored so a stray one can never be committed into a fixture.
.enola/

# ---- everything below is about this being a PUBLIC repository ----------
#
# Audited 2026-09-21: no secret has ever been committed, at HEAD or in any
# reachable commit. `.env` -- which holds the Aura password, the OpenAI key,
# the Daytona key and the Mistral key -- has been ignored since the first
# commit. The one history hit for `neo4j+s://` was a placeholder in a
# document that no longer exists. What follows keeps it that way and keeps
# the repository small.

# CREDENTIALS, in every spelling. `.env` alone was the rule; a single
# `.env.local` or a key dropped next to it would have been committed.
.env*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!**/*.pub
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
!id_rsa*.pub
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
!id_ed25519*.pub
*.p12
*.pfx
.netrc
*credentials*.json
*service-account*.json

# THE AGENTS' OWN CONSOLE OUTPUT. Every prompt, every tool result and every
# traceback of a run, tens of megabytes each, and regenerable by rerunning.
# The event log is the record that matters and it is already ignored above.
runs/experiment-*.log
runs/*.log
# ...and at any depth: runs/contaminated/oapi-01.log was missed by the
# single-level glob above.
runs/**/*.log

# TREE TARBALLS. One snapshot of the agent's whole checkout per attempt --
# 770 KB each, twenty per run, and `runs/packages/<id>.tgz` is a second copy
# of the lot. The derived documents beside them (series, metrics, attempts,
# the final procedure, MANIFEST) are small, are what a deck reads, and stay
# tracked.
runs/**/*.tgz
runs/**/trees/

# Scratch and editor state.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Local Cognee state: SQLite users and datasets, the LanceDB vector index,
# and its logs. Machine-local, large, and it is the store the graph is
# joined against -- a copy in the repository would be a second, divergent
# memory.
.cognee/
.cognee_system/
**/.cognee_system/
*.lance/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# Archived experiment directories, kept out of the way by hand.
archive/

# SUPERSEDED EXPERIMENT OUTPUT. All of it DERIVED -- each line names the
# script that regenerates it -- and all of it from series this project has
# moved past. Ignored rather than deleted: the files stay on disk, the deck
# serves `runs/` from disk rather than from git, and nothing in the repo
# reads them except the generator itself.
#
# NOT dead code. Audited 2026-09-21: every module in orchestrator/, swarm/
# and harness/ is imported by something live, including the 1,301-line
# orchestrator/run.py that swarm/run.py imports from. There is nothing
# code-shaped here to ignore, and nothing code-shaped has been.
# A trailing `# comment` is NOT a comment here -- the whole line, spaces
# and hash included, becomes the pattern. Written that way first, and
# `git check-ignore` said NOT IGNORED for both of these.
#
# regenerated by scripts/plot_series.py
runs/plots/
# regenerated by scripts/export_demo.py
runs/demo-export/
# the September 19 and 20 series; already covered by runs/*-artifacts/
# above, named here so the intent is not an accident of that rule
runs/exp2-artifacts/
runs/exp3-artifacts/

# ---- the lab notebook, and the lab's own output --------------------------
#
# This repository is what someone needs to CLONE AND RUN AN EXPERIMENT. It is
# not the record of the experiments already run here. Everything below stays
# on disk and out of the public tree.
#
# The notes and specs: a day-by-day lab notebook, the hard-won list, two
# dated audits and a spec describing a fixture that no longer exists. The
# README carries what a user actually needs; code comments still cite
# NOTES-hard-won.md by name, and that is a pointer into the author's own
# copy rather than a promise the file is here.
NOTES.md
NOTES-hard-won.md
FIXES-*.md
AUDIT-*.md
REDESIGN-*.md
miss-slaytona-forje-spec.md

# Every run this harness has produced: event logs, archives, packaged
# demos, per-attempt trees, the cross-run table and the plots. A user's own
# runs land here and are theirs, not ours.
runs/

# Skills distilled BY past runs, kept for comparison. The seed skill that a
# run actually needs -- skills/pydantic-v2-migration/SKILL.md, read by
# cognee_layer.SEED_SKILL_PATH -- stays tracked.
skills/live-archive-*/

# MACHINE-LOCAL STATE, and shipping it is worse than shipping nothing.
#
# `.snapshot_state-<fixture>.json` records a Daytona snapshot -- its mode,
# cpu, memory and the fixture hash it was built from -- and `swarm/run.py`
# requires one (`load_state()` raises without it). But the snapshot it names
# lives in the account that built it. A cloner running against ours would
# get a confusing Daytona failure; with the file absent they get the error
# that tells them what to do: "run scripts/build_snapshot.py first".
.snapshot_state-*.json

# Read by nothing. Grepped the whole tree: no module, script or shell file
# mentions it.
.freeze-hash
